# Easy Read versions: micro[220:], all macro, all competency.
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
EASY = {
    'micro::Identify the DLP by name in week one':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Find out who deals with child protection in your service. This person is the DLP.\n• Find out the DLP and the deputy for every school you visit.\n• Write down their names and phone numbers by the end of week one.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• When you are worried about a child, you have minutes to act, not days.\n• If you cannot reach any DLP, you still tell Tusla yourself. Then you tell the DLP after.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The names are written down, not looked up later.\n• You can find them without a laptop.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• DLP: Designated Liaison Person. The person in a school or service who deals with child protection worries.\n• Deputy: the person who stands in when the DLP is away.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency.',
    'micro::Learn the referral and risk pathways':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Learn where to send a child when they need help you cannot give.\n• This might be for self-harm, mental illness, disability or a family crisis.\n• For each route, learn who can refer, how, and how long the wait may be.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Knowing a route exists is not enough.\n• You need to know what happens next, so you can tell the family.\n• If you get it wrong, the referral is turned down and the family waits for nothing.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You know what happens after you refer.\n• You know the emergency route when a child may be at risk.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Referral: asking another service to help a child.\n• Criteria: the rules a service uses to decide who it will see.\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.\n• GP: family doctor.',
    'micro::Learn the records, IT and information governance policy':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Learn your service's rules for keeping records safe.\n• Learn where records are kept, who can see them and how long they are kept.\n• Learn what to do if a record is lost or sent to the wrong person.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Psychology records are very private. The law protects them.\n• A lost notebook or wrong email is a breach. It must be reported.\n• Rules differ between services, so check.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You know where records live and for how long.\n• If data is lost, you tell your supervisor and the Data Protection Officer at once.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Data breach: when private information is lost or seen by the wrong person.\n• Data Protection Officer: the person in charge of keeping personal data safe.\n• GDPR: General Data Protection Regulation. The law on personal information.",
    'micro::Ask which tools you may administer independently here':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Ask which tests you may give on your own in this placement.\n• Get this agreed in writing in your Placement Plan in the first two weeks.\n• Write down how much help you need with each test.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Test makers limit who can use their tests. Services have their own rules too.\n• If you give a test you were not cleared for, the scores may not be usable.\n• What you could do on your last placement may not carry over.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The list is agreed in writing in the Placement Plan.\n• If you are asked to give a test not on the list, you say so.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Placement Plan: the written plan for what you will do on placement.\n• Supervisor: the qualified psychologist who guides and checks your work.',
    'micro::Explain purpose, process and who will see the report':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Before you start, explain why you are involved and what you will do.\n• Say how long it will take and what will be written.\n• Say who will read the report. Check the person understands. Then ask for consent.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Consent only counts if the person knows what they are agreeing to.\n• Parents often guess wrong about who sees the report.\n• If they find out later, trust is hard to win back.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You explain in plain words, before anything begins.\n• If a parent did not understand, you stop and explain again. They can agree again or say no.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Consent: saying yes to something after it has been clearly explained.\n• Informed consent: consent given when you know all the important facts.',
    'micro::State the right to stop or withdraw':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Tell every parent and child that they can stop at any time.\n• Tell them that stopping will not change the help they get.\n• Use words they understand. Write down that you said it.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Consent goes on the whole time. It is not a one-off.\n• Families often feel they cannot say no to a psychologist.\n• If they do not know they can stop, their yes is not truly free.\n• Families who know they can stop are more likely to stay.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You said it clearly enough that people could act on it.\n• If someone stops, you accept it without pressure and leave the door open.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Withdraw: to stop taking part.\n• Consent: saying yes after something has been clearly explained.',
    'micro::State the limits of confidentiality before disclosure':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Explain what you cannot keep private. Do this at the start.\n• Use words that suit the child's age.\n• Do this before any talk where a child might tell you something serious.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Some things must be passed on. This includes serious risk of harm, child protection worries, or a court order.\n• A young person who learns this too late may feel let down.\n• They may not trust another adult again.\n• Explaining first lets them choose what to say.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You explain the limits first, not after.\n• You never promise that everything stays in the room.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Confidentiality: keeping what someone tells you private.\n• Disclosure: when someone tells you about harm or risk.",
    'micro::Establish who holds guardianship before contact':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Before you meet a child, find out who their legal guardians are.\n• Find out whose consent you need.\n• Write this down.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The person who brings the child is not always the one who can give consent.\n• A mother is always a guardian. Some fathers are and some are not.\n• For a child in care, Tusla may hold the right to consent.\n• The rules are detailed, so check with your service.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You checked. You did not assume.\n• If you find out later that a guardian was missed, you stop. You tell your supervisor and contact that guardian.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Guardian: a person with legal rights and duties for a child.\n• Consent: saying yes after something has been clearly explained.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency.',
    'micro::Use supported decision-making where capacity is in question':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• With a young adult, start by believing they can make their own decision.\n• Give them all the help you can to decide.\n• Write down how you helped them.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• An Irish law says adults are presumed able to decide.\n• A choice you think is unwise does not prove they cannot decide.\n• Ability is judged one decision at a time.\n• If you assume they cannot decide, you take away a right.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your notes say how you supported the decision, not just that you did.\n• You do not just take a carer's word for an adult who was never asked.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Capacity: being able to understand, remember and weigh up information, then make and share a choice.\n• Supported decision-making: helping a person to make their own choice.",
    'micro::Recognise and respond to non-verbal withdrawal':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Notice when a child wants to stop but does not say it.\n• They may turn away, go quiet, put their head down or look at the door.\n• Check in with them. If it goes on, stop. Write down what you saw.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Many children will not say stop out loud.\n• Young children and children with communication needs may have no other way to tell you.\n• Carrying on goes against the child's wishes.\n• The results will not be valid.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You stopped.\n• You do not treat silence as being difficult.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Assent: a child's own agreement to take part.\n• Valid: results you can trust.",
    'micro::Obtain assent from the child in their own language':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Ask every child if they agree to take part.\n• Use their own language. This could be their home language, Irish, sign language or a talking device.\n• Use words that suit their age. Write down their answer.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Children have the right to give their views on things that affect them.\n• A yes to words they did not understand is not a real yes.\n• A child who was never asked has been done to, not worked with.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The child was asked.\n• If a child says no, you respect it for now. You find out why and plan with the family.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Assent: a child's own agreement to take part.\n• AAC: Augmentative and Alternative Communication. Ways to talk without speech.\n• UNCRC: United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child.",
    'micro::Record what was explained, not just that consent was given':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• After each consent talk, write a note.\n• Say what you explained: why, what you will do, who sees the report, what you cannot keep private, and the right to stop.\n• Say who was there, what they asked and what they said.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A tick in a box proves nothing about what the person was told.\n• If there is a complaint, the question is what they were told.\n• A good note protects the family, the child and you.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The note shows what was said.\n• If a note is too thin, you add a dated note. You mark it as written later.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Consent: saying yes after something has been clearly explained.\n• Confidentiality: keeping what someone tells you private.',
    'micro::Recognise a concern against the threshold':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Notice signs that a child may be harmed.\n• Name the type of harm. Check it against the level set in the national guidance.\n• Act on the same day.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Worries rarely come as a clear story from the child.\n• They may come as an injury, hunger, fear, a drawing or a change in behaviour.\n• Read the list of signs in the national guidance, not a summary.\n• Missing a sign leaves a child unprotected. Seeing it and not acting is worse.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You knew it when you saw it.\n• You do not explain a sign away because the family seemed nice.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Threshold: the level of worry at which you must act.\n• Children First: Ireland's national guidance and law on keeping children safe.",
    'micro::Do not investigate or question further':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• If a child tells you about harm, listen and reassure them.\n• Only say things like tell me more, if needed.\n• Do not promise to keep it secret. Tell them what happens next.\n• Do not ask lots of questions.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Finding out the facts is the job of Tusla and the Gardaí. It is not your job.\n• Leading questions can change the child's story. They can harm a later case.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You did not question the child.\n• If you asked a leading question, you write down exactly what you asked. You tell the DLP and Tusla.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Leading question: a question that suggests the answer.\n• Gardaí: the Irish police.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency.\n• DLP: Designated Liaison Person. The person who deals with child protection worries.",
    'micro::Record verbatim what was said or seen, at the time':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Write down the child's exact words.\n• Write down what you saw and what you said.\n• Do it as soon as you can. Add the date and time, and sign it.\n• Keep facts apart from your own opinion.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Tusla, the Gardaí or a court may read your record.\n• A note made at the time, in the child's words, carries weight.\n• A summary written days later does not.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your record was made at the time and sticks to the facts.\n• If you could not write it then, you note when you wrote it and why it was late.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Verbatim: word for word.\n• Gardaí: the Irish police.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency.",
    'micro::Act the same day without waiting for supervision':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Act on a child protection worry the same day.\n• Tell the DLP. If it meets the level for reporting, tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly.\n• Do this even if you cannot reach your supervisor.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• As a mandated person, the law says you must report as soon as you can.\n• Your next supervision may be days away.\n• Supervision is for support after you act. It is not permission before.\n• If your supervisor is free that day, talk to them. But the worry sets the timing.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The duty does not wait.\n• If you delayed, you act now and write down the delay honestly.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Mandated person: a person the law says must report child protection worries.\n• DLP: Designated Liaison Person. The person who deals with child protection worries.',
    'micro::Report to the DLP and record the time':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Tell the DLP about a child protection worry.\n• Write down who you told and when.\n• Make sure a report goes to Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. It can be made jointly with the DLP, or by you.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The DLP helps the service respond in the same way each time.\n• Telling the DLP is not enough on its own. You still have your own duty to report.\n• Your note of who and when shows the duty was met.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The file shows who and when.\n• If you cannot confirm the Tusla report was made, you make it yourself. Then you write that down.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• DLP: Designated Liaison Person. The person in a school or service who deals with child protection worries.\n• Joint report: one report made together that meets both people's duty.",
    'micro::Bring it to supervision afterwards, not instead':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• After any child protection action, bring it to your next supervision.\n• Bring your record.\n• Think about your choices and how it affected you.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• This work is upsetting. The choices are rarely clear.\n• Supervision after is where you check your thinking and learn.\n• The order matters. Waiting for supervision instead of acting is a breach.\n• Acting with no supervision after leaves you carrying it alone.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Supervision follows the action. It does not replace it.\n• If a case stays with you, you raise it again. You can also use staff support services.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Supervision: regular meetings with a senior psychologist to guide your work.\n• Breach: breaking a rule or duty.',
    'micro::Identify the issue and all affected parties':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• When you face a hard choice about right and wrong, name the problem in one sentence.\n• List everyone it affects. Include people who are easy to forget.\n• This is step 1 of the ethics steps in the PSI Code.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• People often think only of the child and parent.\n• A brother or sister, a teacher, the service or you may be affected too.\n• If you miss someone, their needs are never weighed.\n• Most poor decisions go wrong at this first step.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your list includes people you would otherwise miss.\n• If someone turns up late, you go back through the later steps.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Ethical dilemma: a hard choice about the right thing to do.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.\n• Code: the PSI rules for how psychologists should act.',
    'micro::Scan the Code for the clauses that apply':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Look through the PSI Code for the parts that fit your problem.\n• Write down each part by its number.\n• Include the parts that pull against the choice you like best.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Numbers let others check your thinking.\n• They show your choice comes from the Code, not just your own values.\n• Hard choices often come from two parts of the Code that pull different ways.\n• You only see this once both are written down.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You give clause numbers.\n• If the Code gives no answer, you say so and move on to the next steps.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.\n• Code: the PSI rules for how psychologists should act.\n• Clause: one numbered part of the Code.',
    "micro::Evaluate each party's rights and responsibilities":
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Look at each person affected, one at a time.\n• Think about their rights, their duties and their wellbeing.\n• Write it down. This is step 3 of the PSI ethics steps.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Taking each person on their own stops the loudest voice taking over.\n• It shows where needs really clash, and where they only seem to.\n• A child's privacy and a parent's right to know can both be real at once.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Each person is thought about on their own.\n• You do not treat the parent's view as the child's view.\n• If rights cannot be matched, you say whose right is limited and why.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Rights: what a person is owed.\n• Wellbeing: how well and happy a person is.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.",
    'micro::Generate as many alternative courses as possible':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• List more than two ways forward before you judge any of them.\n• Include the less obvious ones.\n• This is step 4 of the PSI ethics steps.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Under pressure, a hard choice can shrink to two options, like tell or do not tell.\n• The best choice is often a third one. You could involve the young person, wait a short time, or ask for advice first.\n• More options often make the choice easier to manage.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You list more than two options.\n• If every option is bad, you pick the least harmful one and write down why.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Option: one possible way forward.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.',
    'micro::Evaluate the likely outcome of each':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• For each option, think about what would likely happen next.\n• Think about each person, now and later on.\n• Write it down. This is step 5 of the PSI ethics steps.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Here the earlier steps come together.\n• The option that feels right in the moment may have hidden costs.\n• The reverse can also be true.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You have thought the results through.\n• You do not just pick what eases your own discomfort now.\n• If you cannot predict results, you pick the option that is easiest to undo. You set a date to review it.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Consequence: what happens because of a choice.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.',
    'micro::Decide and record the decision':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Make the decision.\n• On the same day, write down what you decided and why.\n• Write down the options you did not choose.\n• This is step 6 of the PSI ethics steps.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A written record shows the decision was thought through.\n• It helps if the decision is questioned later.\n• A decision never written down cannot be shown to have been made.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• It is written down, with the reasons.\n• If you did not write it at the time, you write it now. You mark it as written later.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Record: a written note kept on file.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.',
    'micro::Take responsibility for the consequences':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• After a decision, look at how it turned out.\n• Own the part that was yours.\n• Act to put right any harm. This is step 7 of the PSI ethics steps.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A careful decision can still go badly.\n• What matters then is honesty. Say so, fix what you can and learn.\n• Following the steps is not an excuse for a bad result.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You take responsibility, including when it went wrong.\n• If the harm is serious, you tell your supervisor at once. You follow service rules.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Responsibility: owning what you did and what came of it.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.',
    'micro::Keep notes at each stage':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Keep dated notes at each of the seven ethics steps.\n• Write them as you go, not at the end.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The PSI Code strongly recommends notes at every step.\n• Notes made as you go show how your thinking grew.\n• Notes written after the result is known can look like you knew the answer all along.\n• They protect you and help with your Appendix 8 entries.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You have notes for each step.\n• If a step has no notes, you write what you remember. You date it and mark it as later.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.\n• Appendix 8: the course form where you write up ethical dilemmas.',
    'micro::Know your publisher competence level for every tool':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• For each test, know the level the test maker says a user needs.\n• Know who holds that level for your use of it. This is usually your supervisor.\n• Know what supervision has been agreed.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Test makers limit who can use each test.\n• Their level systems differ, so check each test. Do not assume.\n• Without this, you cannot tell when you are going beyond what you are allowed to do.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can state the level for each test.\n• If you used a test beyond your level, you tell your supervisor. You decide together if the scores can be used.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Publisher: the company that makes and sells a test.\n• Restricted test: a test only some qualified people may use.',
    'micro::Say early what you are not yet competent to do':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Tell your supervisor early what you cannot do well yet.\n• Do this when work is given out, before you are asked.\n• Suggest how the gap can be covered. This could be working together, supervision or training.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Supervisors give you work based on what they think you can do.\n• If you do not say, they will assume you can.\n• The gap may then show up in front of a family or school.\n• Saying it early is professional. Saying it after is too late.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You speak up before being asked.\n• If you find a gap mid-case, you say so straight away.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Competent: able to do something well and safely.\n• Supervisor: the qualified psychologist who guides and checks your work.',
    'micro::Decline work that exceeds you':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Say no to work that is beyond your training.\n• Say it clearly and politely.\n• Offer another way if you can. Write it down.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Sometimes saying you have a gap is not enough. The right answer is no.\n• This may be very hard work, or work with no one to supervise you.\n• Saying no protects the child from work that is not good enough.\n• A trainee who says yes to everything is a risk.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You said no.\n• If you are pushed, you repeat the limit. You explain the risk to the child and go to your supervisor.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Competence: having the skills to do a task safely.\n• Decline: politely say no.',
    'micro::Refer on and maintain responsibility until contact is made':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• When you refer a child to another service, check they got the referral.\n• Keep following up until that service has made contact.\n• Only then hand the child over.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A referral is a handover. It is not an exit.\n• Referrals get lost, turned down or stuck on waiting lists. Risk can change.\n• If you close the file too early, the child can fall into a gap.\n• That gap is where harm can go unnoticed.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You did not drop the child at referral.\n• If a referral is lost or turned down, you tell the family. You find another route.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Referral: asking another service to help a child.\n• Follow up: check back to see what has happened.',
    'micro::Include a limitations paragraph in every report':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Put a short section on limits in every report.\n• Say what your report cannot show.\n• Include limits in your own skills where they matter.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Every report has limits.\n• A report that states none is either very complete or not honest. Readers cannot tell which.\n• This skill is about doing it every time, not only for hard cases.\n• The same general wording pasted into every report is not enough.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Every report has its own limits section.\n• If a report went out without one, you send an addition if it matters.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Limitations: what a report or test cannot tell us.\n• Addendum: an extra part added to a report after it was sent.',
    'micro::Track your actual caseload and travel load':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Keep a weekly record of your workload.\n• Count your cases, your driving time, your writing time and your meetings.\n• Know your numbers without looking.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• In rural Ireland you may drive a long way between schools.\n• A caseload can look fine on paper but be too much in real life.\n• Clear numbers are harder to ignore than just saying you are overwhelmed.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You know the numbers.\n• If you have too much, you bring the numbers to supervision. You ask for help to plan what comes first.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Caseload: the children and families you are working with.\n• Workload: all the work you have to do, including travel and writing.',
    'micro::Recognise your own warning signs':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Know your own early signs of stress and burnout.\n• These might be poor sleep, getting cross, dread before work, or cutting corners.\n• Check for them often, and act on them.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Burnout builds slowly.\n• The person going through it is often the last to see it.\n• Knowing your signs lets you act before your judgement suffers.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can name your signs.\n• If they do not ease, you tell your supervisor. You use a support service and see your GP.\n• You do not just push on to the end of placement.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Burnout: being worn out by work, so you feel empty and care less.\n• GP: family doctor.',
    'micro::Raise workload before it becomes a problem':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Watch your workload numbers.\n• When you see a busy time coming, tell your supervisor early.\n• Bring a clear idea of what could change.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Raised early, too much work can be sorted. Tasks can be moved, delayed or shared.\n• Raised late, deadlines are missed. Families and schools are let down.\n• Trainees often wait because they fear looking weak. Supervisors would rather hear early.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You raise it before, not after.\n• If nothing changes, you raise it again in writing. You can involve your university tutor.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Workload: all the work you have to do.\n• Tutor: the university staff member who supports you on placement.',
    'micro::Seek supervision when stressed, not only when stuck':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Bring stress to supervision, not only problems with cases.\n• Do this when the stress is there.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The PSI Code says you must seek support. It is required, not just allowed.\n• Stress affects your judgement, your records and how you are with families.\n• If you keep it to yourself, your supervisor cannot see the risk.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You talk about stress in supervision.\n• If supervision does not feel safe for this, you use your university tutor or another route.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Supervision: regular meetings with a senior psychologist to guide your work.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.',
    'micro::Know the seven-day notification duty':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Read section 5 of the UCD Handbook. It sets out a duty to notify within seven days.\n• Write down in your own words what starts the duty.\n• Write down who you tell, how and by when.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The details were not in the sources for this entry. Do not act on a summary.\n• Duties like this often cover changes that affect whether you are fit to practise.\n• Missing the deadline can become a problem in itself.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You could act on it if needed.\n• If you missed the deadline, you tell them at once and explain honestly.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• UCD: University College Dublin.\n• Handbook: the course rule book.\n• Fit to practise: safe and able to do the job.',
    'micro::Refrain from practice where judgement is seriously impaired':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Know when your judgement is badly affected.\n• Illness, grief, tiredness, medicine, alcohol or a crisis at home can all do this.\n• Then stop seeing families. Tell your supervisor and tutor.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The PSI Code says a psychologist whose judgement is badly affected should stop working.\n• This is hard, because it affects the very judgement you would use to notice.\n• So know your signs in advance and have a plan.\n• Listen if your supervisor sees it before you do.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You would know, and you would act.\n• If you worked while unwell, you tell your supervisor. You check and fix the work.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Impaired judgement: when your thinking is not clear enough to work safely.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.',
    'micro::Know the 30 minutes per placement day entitlement':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Know the rule on supervision time.\n• You are due 30 minutes of supervision for each day on placement.\n• At four days a week, that is two hours a week. Work it out for your own days.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• If you do not know the figure, you cannot see when you get less.\n• It is a rule, not a favour you have to ask for.\n• Read the standard itself, so you quote its real words.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can state it calmly if the hour slips.\n• If the total falls short, you raise it with dates. You suggest a plan to make it up.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Supervision: regular meetings with a senior psychologist to guide your work.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.\n• Accreditation standard: a rule a course must meet to be approved.',
    'micro::Know the four components expected':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Know the four parts of supervision.\n• They are teaching, support, judging your standard and keeping people safe.\n• Give an example of each.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Supervision is not just one thing.\n• If you only know the teaching part, you may miss the rest.\n• No one may check how you are, tell you your standard or look at risk.\n• Read section 6.1 of the Handbook for the exact words.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can name the four parts.\n• When one is missing, you ask for it on the next agenda.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Educative: teaching.\n• Supportive: helping you cope.\n• Evaluative: judging how well you are doing.\n• Safeguarding: keeping children and others safe.',
    'micro::Bring a draft to week one':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Bring a first draft of your supervision contract to week one.\n• Make it fit this service.\n• Use it to shape your first supervision meeting.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Week one is the easiest time to ask for things. Later it can feel like a complaint.\n• With a draft, you talk about your terms. You do not just sign a form.\n• Writers on supervision see this as the first step, not just paperwork.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You arrived with something.\n• If week one passes with no contract, you raise it at the next meeting and bring your draft.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Supervision contract: a written agreement about how supervision will work.\n• Draft: a first version.',
    'micro::Record day, time, duration and format':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Write down the exact day, time and length of supervision.\n• Write down if it is in person or online, and where.\n• Put it in both calendars as a repeat meeting before you leave the room.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Weekly is not a clear promise. Tuesday 10 to 11, in person, is.\n• A vague hour is the first thing to move when work gets busy.\n• Then there is nothing to point to.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The plan is specific, not as arranged.\n• If the time does not work, you agree a new fixed time. You update the contract.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Format: how a meeting happens, like in person or by video.\n• Supervision contract: a written agreement about how supervision will work.',
    'micro::Agree how cancellations are handled':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Agree what happens if supervision is cancelled.\n• Agree who rearranges it and by when.\n• A missed hour should be moved to the same week, not dropped.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Supervision is rarely lost all at once. It slips bit by bit.\n• A clear rule stops that slide.\n• Without it, each cancellation is argued again, and the trainee usually gives in.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You know who rearranges, and by when.\n• When the hour is cancelled, you reply that day with two new times in the same week.\n• If the rule is ignored, you raise it with dates.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Cancellation: when a planned meeting is called off.\n• Clause: one part of a written agreement.',
    'micro::Both sign and date it':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Make sure you and your supervisor both sign and date the contract.\n• Do this in the first week.\n• Keep a copy where you and the university can find it.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• An unsigned contract is only a draft.\n• A draft carries no weight when you need it.\n• A signed contract shows the university what was agreed if a problem comes up.\n• Check which form the university wants.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• It is signed.\n• If it was never signed, you get it signed now with the real date. You do not backdate it.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Backdate: put an earlier date on something than the day it was signed.\n• Supervision contract: a written agreement about how supervision will work.',
    'micro::Protect the hour':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Keep your weekly supervision hour happening.\n• If it moves, rebook it in the same week.\n• Use it for supervision, not just for running cases.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The weekly hour is one of the most protective parts of your placement.\n• It is also the first thing to disappear.\n• It goes bit by bit, and each small loss seems fair.\n• You may find near the end that it has not been happening.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• It happened.\n• If it has lapsed, you count the missed dates. You agree a plan in writing to make it up.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Supervision: regular meetings with a senior psychologist to guide your work.\n• Lapse: stop happening.',
    'micro::Raise it if it slips twice':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• If supervision is cancelled twice in a row, raise it at the next meeting.\n• Stay calm. Bring the dates and the rule.\n• If nothing changes, contact university placement staff. Read the Handbook for how.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Two cancellations in a row is a pattern.\n• Supervision is a course rule, not a kindness.\n• It can fall short without anyone being bad. The service may just be stretched.\n• Trainees often blame themselves and wait. By the end, nothing can be fixed.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You treat it as a rule, not a favour.\n• You keep working safely. This may mean waiting before taking on new complex work.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Supervision: regular meetings with a senior psychologist to guide your work.\n• UCD: University College Dublin.',
    'micro::Keep to 30 minutes per placement day cumulatively':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Keep a running total of your supervision time from day one.\n• Count the formal hour and the informal chats.\n• Know at any time if you are ahead or behind.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The rule is 30 minutes for each placement day.\n• Informal contact counts too, like a call after a hard visit. But it only counts if you log it.\n• At the end, your log is the proof the rule was met.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You track the total.\n• If you fall behind, you show the figures early. You suggest extra sessions.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Informal supervision: short, unplanned talks with your supervisor about your work.\n• Log: a written list of what happened and when.\n• Cumulative: added up over time.',
    'micro::Send it the day before':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Send your supervision agenda by email the day before each session.\n• Send it at a regular time.\n• Act on anything urgent at once. Do not save it for supervision.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Your supervisor comes ready, and the hour starts with what matters.\n• Sending it as you walk in loses that benefit.\n• The email also keeps a dated record of what you raised.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• It is sent in advance, not on arrival.\n• You never hold a child protection worry for supervision. You tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Then you bring it to supervision.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Agenda: a list of things to talk about at a meeting.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency.',
    'micro::Keep it to three items':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Keep your agenda to about three main items.\n• For each one, show what you already thought and what you want to ask.\n• Put them in order of importance.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• An hour can only hold about three real items.\n• A long list becomes a list read aloud.\n• The hard item gets pushed off the end.\n• Updates that could be an email should be an email.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your agenda is focused.\n• If more than three things need supervision, you put them in order. You email the rest or ask for more time.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Agenda: a list of things to talk about at a meeting.\n• Priority: how important something is.',
    'micro::Update on actions agreed last time':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• End each agenda with last week's actions.\n• Say honestly where each one is at.\n• If an action keeps not getting done, talk about why.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• It links each session to the last one, so learning builds up.\n• It gives you a record of change for your reflections and essay.\n• Without it, agreed actions quietly drop.\n• The same problem comes back weeks later as if it were new.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You closed the loop.\n• If actions keep slipping, you check if they were too big or too vague. You redesign them.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Action: a task agreed at a meeting.\n• Agenda: a list of things to talk about at a meeting.",
    'micro::Include at least one thing that went badly':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Put at least one thing that went badly on every agenda.\n• Raise it without being asked.\n• Follow through on what it needs.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The item you want to avoid is usually the most useful one.\n• Research found trainees often hold back mistakes from supervisors.\n• If you do not write it down, you will not raise it.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your agenda has more than successes.\n• If you missed a child protection worry, you act first. You tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the DLP is not enough on its own.\n• Then you bring it to supervision.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• DLP: Designated Liaison Person. The person who deals with child protection worries.\n• Agenda: a list of things to talk about at a meeting.',
    'micro::Complete a record for every session':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Fill in a supervision record for every session.\n• Do it on the same day.\n• Include logged informal contact too.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• These records are marked by the course.\n• They are also the only proof that supervision happened.\n• On the day it takes about ten minutes. Written months later, it is plain it was made up after.\n• Count your own weeks to know how many records you need.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You have about one record per week. That is about 19 for a 75-day placement.\n• If records are missing, you write them now. You mark them honestly as late.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Appendix 8: the course form for supervision records.\n• Contemporaneous: written at the time.',
    'micro::Record topics, actions and who does what':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• In each supervision record, write what was decided, not just what was talked about.\n• For each action, name who does it, what they do and by when.\n• Use case numbers, never names.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A list of topics shows supervision happened.\n• A list of decisions shows it changed something.\n• Vague actions, like think about the case, cannot be checked.\n• Clear records stop arguments about who agreed to what.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your actions are specific.\n• If you both remember a decision differently, you check the record and fix it together.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Action: a task agreed at a meeting.\n• Supervision record: notes of each supervision meeting.',
    'micro::Get them co-signed':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Get your supervisor to sign each supervision record.\n• Do it within about a month of the session.\n• Ask for missing signatures without feeling awkward.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A record signed by both of you is evidence.\n• A record signed only by you is just a claim.\n• Signing each month is easy. A pile of unsigned records at the end is a problem.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Records are signed, not just written.\n• If signatures are missing at the end, you list them and agree a date. You tell the university if they cannot be got.\n• You never sign for anyone else.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Co-sign: both people sign.\n• Countersign: a senior person signs to confirm your record.',
    'micro::Tag which of the four components the session covered':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Mark each supervision record with the parts it covered.\n• The four parts are teaching, support, judging your standard and keeping people safe.\n• Notice when a part has been missing for weeks.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Marking takes no time on the day.\n• Later you can quickly see if you ever got feedback on your standard.\n• Without marks, a gap stays hidden.\n• Marking all four every time shows nothing.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You use the tags E, S, Ev and Sg correctly.\n• When a part is missing, you ask for it on the next agenda.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• E: educative, or teaching.\n• S: supportive, or helping you cope.\n• Ev: evaluative, or judging how you are doing.\n• Sg: safeguarding, or keeping people safe.',
    'micro::Record any ethical dilemma using the seven steps':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Notice when you face an ethical dilemma.\n• Write it up using all seven steps in the PSI Code.\n• Copy the steps from the Code, not from memory. Finish the entry once you know the outcome.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• One good dilemma entry is worth more than twenty routine records.\n• Ethical practice is hard to show any other way.\n• Writing discussed a tricky case shows no thinking.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• All seven steps are there.\n• If the entry shows a poor decision, you say so honestly. You note what you would do differently.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Ethical dilemma: a hard choice about the right thing to do.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.',
    'micro::Arrange it in the first weeks':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Arrange to watch your supervisor at work in your first weeks.\n• Agree the dates.\n• Make sure family consent is in place before you get busy.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The rule says observing goes both ways. You watch them, and they watch you.\n• Trainees often skip watching their supervisor.\n• Asking to watch sometime gets a yes but no date.\n• Your supervisor needs time to get family consent.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• It is done early, as the Handbook expects.\n• If a planned visit falls through, you ask for the next one straight away.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Observe: watch someone work so you can learn.\n• Consent: saying yes after something has been clearly explained.',
    'micro::Agree a focus before observing':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Before you watch your supervisor, pick what you want to learn.\n• Focus on how they handle things, not what the test is.\n• Take notes against that aim.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• You already know how a test should be given.\n• What you do not know is how they manage the room and the hard moments.\n• Without a focus, you watch everything and learn little.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You knew what you were watching for.\n• If that moment does not come up, you watch what did happen. You ask your supervisor how they would have handled it.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Process: how someone does something.\n• Content: what is being done.',
    'micro::Record what they did that you would not have':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• When you watch your supervisor, write down what they did that you would not have done.\n• Use their exact words where you can.\n• Name what you will copy.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The learning is in the gap between you and an expert.\n• Writing she was very good gives you nothing to use.\n• The exact words someone uses in a hard moment are more useful than a general rule.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your notes are specific, not just praise.\n• If you disagree with something, you note it. You raise it politely in the talk after.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Verbatim: word for word.\n• Appendix 7: the course form for observations.',
    'micro::Ask afterwards why they did it that way':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• After watching your supervisor, ask why they did things that way.\n• Do this the same day.\n• Ask about specific choices. Write down their reasons.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Experts often act without putting it into words.\n• They may only explain once asked.\n• If you do not ask, you copy what they did but not why.\n• Then you cannot use it in new situations.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You asked.\n• If they cannot explain, you ask what they noticed first. Or you ask what would have made them act differently.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Debrief: a talk after an event about what happened.\n• Observe: watch someone work so you can learn.',
    'micro::Arrange both observations early':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Plan the times your supervisor will watch you work.\n• In the first month, book and date the first one.\n• Plan the second with time to act on the feedback.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• This is the only time your supervisor sees what you really do.\n• In other talks, they only hear your account.\n• Feedback in the last two weeks leaves no time to act on it.\n• Read the Handbook for the exact number and timing.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• It is booked in the first month.\n• If one is cancelled, you rebook it there and then. You record the new date.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Observation: when someone watches you work.\n• Feedback: comments on how you did.',
    'micro::Request a focus you find hard':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Before you are watched, choose a focus you find hard.\n• Write it down.\n• Make sure it is something the watcher can see.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Any feedback welcome tends to bring only praise.\n• A clear focus brings something you can use.\n• Choosing a hard focus shows you lead your own learning.\n• Being watched on what you already do well wastes the chance.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your focus is not giving a test, which is the easiest part.\n• If the hard moment does not happen, you say so. You keep the same focus next time.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Focus: the one thing you want feedback on.\n• Feedback: comments on how you did.',
    'micro::Receive critical feedback without defending':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Listen to hard feedback without arguing or saying sorry.\n• Write it down as it was said.\n• Ask one question to check you understand. Come back to it next time.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Arguing and falling apart both cost you the same thing. You lose the information.\n• If you argue every point, your supervisor will give less feedback.\n• Supervisors may see defending as a problem with using supervision.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You listened.\n• If you catch yourself arguing, you stop and say you will think about it.\n• If you still disagree a week later, you bring your reasons calmly.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Critical feedback: comments about what did not go well.\n• Defensive: quick to argue or explain away.',
    'micro::Act on the feedback and say so next time':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• After feedback, try one clear change in your work.\n• Do it within a week or two.\n• Tell your supervisor without being asked.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Coming back with what you tried shows you used the feedback.\n• The top grade asks for a real change after being watched.\n• If you nod and change nothing, the next feedback will say the same thing.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Something changed.\n• The record shows before and after.\n• If the change did not work, you report that too. You ask what else to try.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Feedback: comments on how you did.\n• Observation: when someone watches you work.',
    'micro::Know what counts as immediate here':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Know the three kinds of worry that cannot wait for supervision.\n• They are a crisis, a worry about someone's welfare, and an ethical dilemma.\n• Raise them the same day.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Trainees often do not want to interrupt. Or they are unsure it is serious enough.\n• Here is a simple test. If you are asking if it is serious enough, it is.\n• An extra call costs minutes. Waiting can cost a child's safety.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can name the three kinds.\n• If you held something back, you raise it now. You record honestly when you knew and when you raised it.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Welfare: a person's safety and wellbeing.\n• Ethical dilemma: a hard choice about the right thing to do.",
    'micro::Know how to reach your supervisor out of session':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Before each school day, have your supervisor's phone number.\n• Know who covers when they are away.\n• Know the school DLP's name and how to reach Tusla. Know which to use first.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Some worries must be raised the same day.\n• Your supervisor may be in another school or on leave.\n• Having only an email wastes time a child may not have.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You have the number.\n• If you reach no one, you still act on your own duty. Tell the DLP, and Tusla if needed. Call the Gardaí if a child is in danger now.\n• You write down every call you tried, with times.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• DLP: Designated Liaison Person. The person who deals with child protection worries.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency.\n• Gardaí: the Irish police.",
    'micro::Act first where the duty is yours':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• When a child protection worry is serious enough, act the same day.\n• Tell the DLP. Tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly, together with the DLP or yourself.\n• Tell your supervisor, even if you could not reach them first.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The law says you must report as soon as you can.\n• Telling the DLP is not enough on its own. You still have your own duty.\n• Supervision helps and supports you. It does not replace the report or give permission.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Supervision informs you. It does not replace your action.\n• If you waited, you act now and write down the delay honestly.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• DLP: Designated Liaison Person. The person who deals with child protection worries.\n• Mandated person: a person the law says must report.',
    'micro::Record the contact and what was agreed':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• After any urgent contact, write it down.\n• Note the time, the person, what was said and what was agreed.\n• If it was a hard choice, write it up in Appendix 8 with your own thoughts.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The record shows the duty was met and the worry was handled.\n• Without it, a well-handled worry looks like one that was ignored.\n• A single line in a day log is lost as evidence.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your record has the time and the content.\n• If an old worry was never written up, you write it now from your notes. You date it today and say it was written later.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Appendix 8: the course form for supervision records and dilemmas.\n• Back-date: put an earlier date on something than when it was written.',
    'micro::Attend consistently':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Go to peer supervision every time. Treat it as fixed.\n• Log each session with the date, the format and a short note.\n• Keep names out of your notes.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Peer supervision is often the first thing dropped when people are busy.\n• Turning up is most of what you give.\n• Sessions only count if you can show they happened.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your attendance is recorded.\n• If the group stops meeting, you offer to set up the next one. You tell your supervisor.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Peer supervision: meeting with other trainees to talk through your work.\n• Anonymised: with names and details taken out so no one can be identified.',
    'micro::Present a case at least once a trimester':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Present one of your own cases to your peer group each term.\n• Take out names and details.\n• Give a clear account and ask one clear question. Write down what you learned.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• You practise explaining a case to people who may challenge you.\n• This helps later in team meetings.\n• Only listening to others does not show you had your own work reviewed.\n• A case you have already solved wastes everyone's time.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You present, not just listen.\n• If you realise you have named a school, you stop. You ask the group not to repeat it. You tell your supervisor that day.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Trimester: one of three terms in the year.\n• Anonymised: with names and details taken out.",
    'micro::Bring a case from an under-covered age band':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Keep track of the age groups you have worked with.\n• Bring a case from an age group you have little of to peer supervision.\n• Log it as a presentation and as proof of range.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The course wants you to work across all age groups.\n• So far, your record shows no early years or young adult work.\n• You cannot add range later. You need to plan it.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• One case counts twice.\n• If no case comes up, you record that it was planned and did not happen. You carry it into the next plan.\n• You never make up cases.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Age band: a group of ages, like early years or young adult.\n• Breadth: working across many kinds of cases.\n• Appendix 1: your Placement Plan form.',
    "micro::Offer something useful to someone else's case":
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Help with other people's cases in peer supervision.\n• Ask questions that open the case up, like what did the child say.\n• Notice when the group agrees too fast, and say so.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A good question often helps more than a quick answer.\n• Have you tried this can close the talk down.\n• A group that agrees too fast stops thinking.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You took part.\n• If a comment lands badly, you say so and ask it as a question. You check in with them after.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Peer supervision: meeting with other trainees to talk through your work.\n• Premature agreement: agreeing too soon, before thinking it through.",
    'micro::Rate all eight before day one, dated':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Before placement starts, rate yourself on all eight areas on the form.\n• Date it before or in the first week.\n• Show it to your supervisor.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• You rate yourself before and after. Comparing the two shows how you grew.\n• This only works if the first rating really came first.\n• A rating written later is plain to see and loses its worth.\n• The ratings are yours to make.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You rated yourself before, for real.\n• If you missed the start, you do it now. You date it truly and say it was late.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Self-evaluation: rating your own skills.\n• Competency: one of the eight areas of skill in the course.\n• Appendix 6: the course form for rating yourself.',
    'micro::Rate honestly, including low':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Rate yourself honestly, even if the rating is low.\n• Base each rating on real proof.\n• Be able to back up each rating with an example.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A low start can feel like failure. But it makes your growth easy to see.\n• If you rate high at the start, you have nothing to show at the end.\n• Supervisors may see a high first rating as poor self-awareness.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your low start shows your path of growth.\n• If your supervisor disagrees, you talk about the proof. Then you decide. You note the talk if you keep your rating.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Self-awareness: knowing your own strengths and weaknesses.\n• Trajectory: the path of your progress over time.',
    'micro::Name two or three priority targets':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Set two or three main goals for the placement.\n• Make each one clear and possible to reach.\n• Link each one to a low rating or an area to work on.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A goal is only useful if you can tell at the end if you met it.\n• Get better at formulation cannot be proved.\n• A goal naming a method, a number of cases and something you will produce can.\n• Too many goals spread your effort thin.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your goals are specific and can be reached.\n• If a goal becomes impossible, you record why. You change it with your supervisor.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Target: a goal you want to reach.\n• Formulation: a shared explanation of why a difficulty is happening.',
    'micro::Carry the targets into the next Placement Plan':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Put your goals into your next Placement Plan.\n• Plan the settings you still need before day one.\n• This includes visits to special education settings.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Goals kept only in your self-rating form do not shape the work you get.\n• Goals in the Placement Plan do.\n• A course rule says you need mainstream primary, secondary and special education.\n• Special education visits must be in the plan before you start.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your goals appear in Appendix 1.\n• If a plan falls through, you agree another one. You change the plan in writing with your supervisor.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Appendix 1: your Placement Plan form.\n• Appendix 6: the course form for rating yourself.\n• Mainstream: an ordinary school, not a special school.',
    'micro::Rate again at the end and compare':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• At the end of placement, rate yourself again.\n• Put the new ratings beside the first ones.\n• Explain each change, and each rating that did not change.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• This is where growth is shown, not just claimed.\n• A rating that did not change can be fine. Explain it, for example with no chance to practise, and say what is next.\n• A rating that is the same with no reason looks like you did not care.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Change is easy to see.\n• If a rating went down, that can be honest. You may now understand the skill better. Say so and give proof.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Comparison: looking at two things side by side.\n• Evidence: proof, such as dated notes or records.',
    'micro::Cover placement, college sessions and independent study':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• In your reflective essay, cover three parts.\n• These are your placement, your college sessions and your own study.\n• Use dated examples. Link them to changes in your work.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The essay turns your forms into one clear story of your year.\n• Read section 4.5b of the Handbook and list what it asks for before you write.\n• If you only write about placement, you miss parts that are required.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• All three parts are there.\n• If one part is thin, you look back at your log and notes. You write honestly about what it gave you.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Reflective essay: writing about what you learned and how you changed.\n• Strand: one part of what the essay must cover.',
    'micro::Name what was hard and what it cost':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• In your essay, name what was hard on placement.\n• Say what it cost you and how you dealt with it.\n• Keep the focus on your work, not your private life.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• An essay where nothing went wrong is hard to believe.\n• Markers may see it as putting on a show.\n• You do not have to share anything personal you do not want to.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You write about real challenges.\n• If writing about a hard moment upsets you more than expected, you stop. You take it to supervision or counselling. You can pick another example.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Reflection: thinking back on what happened and what you learned.\n• Self-care: things you do to look after your own wellbeing.',
    'micro::Reflect on your own culture, values and biases':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• In your essay, name one clear way your own culture, values or beliefs affected your work.\n• Say how you noticed and what changed.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• CORU asks you to think carefully about yourself and your own biases.\n• Supervision can help you with this.\n• Just saying you know you have bias is not enough.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You give a real example from your work.\n• If you cannot find one, look at times you disagreed with a parent or teacher. Or a time your first idea was wrong. Take this to supervision.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Bias: a view you hold that can affect your judgement without you noticing.\n• CORU: Ireland's regulator for health and social care professionals.",
    'micro::Trace a change in practice to a specific reflection':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• In your essay, show one change in your work.\n• Link it back to a dated reflection or piece of feedback.\n• Show what you did before, what you thought about, and what you did after.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Saying I grew in confidence is only a claim.\n• A dated before and after is proof.\n• A short weekly note makes the essay easier. You edit rather than write from memory.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your reflection changed something.\n• If you have no weekly notes, you use your dated records. You say where memory fills gaps. You start the habit now.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Reflection: thinking back on what happened and what you learned.\n• Feedback: comments on how you did.',
    'micro::Name a self-care strategy and evidence it works':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• In your essay, name one self-care activity you really did.\n• Say when you did it.\n• Show what proves it helped.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Burnout builds up over time.\n• I will look after myself is a wish, not a plan.\n• The essay needs a real activity and some proof it helped.\n• What you share is your choice.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You name an activity, not a wish.\n• If it stops working, you raise it in supervision. You use a support service and see your GP. You write honestly about what you learned.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Self-care: things you do to look after your own wellbeing.\n• Burnout: being worn out by work, so you feel empty and care less.\n• GP: family doctor.',
    'micro::Frame an answerable question from a real referral':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Turn a worry in a referral into a clear question you can search.\n• Name the group of children, the support, what you compare it with, and the result you can measure.\n• Write the question down.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A referral comes as a worry, like he cannot read.\n• Searching on the worry gives thousands of results and no answer.\n• A clear question can be answered in about twenty minutes.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your question is clear enough to search.\n• If you find nothing, you widen one part at a time. You note each change.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Referral: a request for a psychologist to help a child.\n• Intervention: a planned way of helping.\n• Outcome: the result you want to see.',
    'micro::Search a database rather than a search engine':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Look first for reviews that sum up many studies.\n• Then search a research database, not a general search engine.\n• Only use a general search engine to find a paper you already know.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• General search engines rank by popularity and adverts, not quality.\n• The first page is often full of sales sites.\n• That is the weak evidence often sold to schools.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You use research databases.\n• If you cannot get access, you note the question and search later. You tell the school your advice will follow.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Database: a searchable collection of research papers.\n• PsycINFO: a database of psychology research.\n• ERIC: a database of education research.\n• EEF: Education Endowment Foundation. It sums up evidence for schools.',
    'micro::Record the search terms and databases used':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Write down how you searched.\n• Note the databases, the date, the exact words you used, how many results, and what you read.\n• Keep a note for each topic.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A search you cannot repeat is a search no one can check.\n• That includes you, six months later.\n• You will be asked the same questions many times in your career.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Someone else could repeat your search.\n• If an old piece of advice rests on a search you did not record, you run it again. You check the answer still holds.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Search terms: the words you type into a database.\n• Reproducible: able to be done again with the same result.',
    'micro::Appraise quality rather than accepting the first result':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Judge how good a study is. Do not just take the first result.\n• Look at how it was done, how many took part, and what it was compared with.\n• Look at the effect size and who paid.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Most search results will not stand up.\n• Studies run by a programme's makers may show bigger effects.\n• If you take the first result, you may suggest something that does not really work.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You judged the study, not just the summary.\n• If advice already given rests on weak evidence, you raise it in supervision.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Appraise: judge how good something is.\n• Abstract: a short summary at the start of a study.\n• Effect size: how big a change a programme made.\n• CASP: Critical Appraisal Skills Programme. Checklists for judging studies.",
    'micro::Judge applicability to this child in this context':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Check if a study fits this child and this school.\n• Compare the children in the study with this child.\n• Check if the school has the staff and time to run it.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A good study shows what worked on average, for those children.\n• It does not prove it will work here.\n• A programme built for groups with a trained tutor may not work in a small school.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You thought about whether it transfers.\n• Your advice says what was changed and why.\n• If a school has changed a programme a lot, you agree which parts must stay. You check if it is still helping.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Applicability: whether something fits this situation.\n• SET: Special Education Teacher.',
    'micro::Log the search as an Appendix 5 research entry':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Write up your search as a research entry in Appendix 5.\n• Include the question, the search, how you judged the studies, what you found, and how it changed your work.\n• Leave out any child or school names.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A search that is not recorded does not count for the research area.\n• On placement, research is mostly shown through this kind of work.\n• Check the Handbook for the exact headings.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• It is recorded.\n• If you have no entries, you pick the search that most changed your advice. You write it up now in your own words.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Appendix 5: the course form for research entries.\n• Research competency: the course skill area about using evidence.',
    'micro::Attach a source to every substantial recommendation':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Give a source for each main piece of advice in a report.\n• This includes programmes, frameworks and claims about what usually works.\n• Keep the list short. Two or three good sources is enough.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A source shows the advice is not just your opinion.\n• It lets readers check it.\n• Advice with no source invites the question says who.\n• You may not be there when it is asked.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• There is a reference list.\n• If a report went out with no source for key advice, you add it at the next review. If it cannot be backed up, you raise it in supervision.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Cite: name the source of an idea.\n• Reference list: the list of sources at the end of a report.',
    'micro::Cite current sources rather than the familiar ones':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Use the newest good sources, not just the ones you know well.\n• Only use older sources for a reason you can give.\n• Check figures from online tools on the day you use them.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Evidence changes. Reviews are updated and advice changes.\n• An old source may mean you suggest something the evidence has moved away from.\n• Readers notice when nothing is recent.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You checked how recent your sources are.\n• If a newer review changes the picture after a report has gone, you raise it in supervision. You decide if the school should be told.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Source: where a piece of information comes from.\n• EEF: Education Endowment Foundation. It sums up evidence for schools.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.',
    'micro::Use APA 7th consistently':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Use one referencing style, APA 7th, in all your writing.\n• Make sure each source in the text is in the list.\n• Make sure each source in the list is used in the text.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Good referencing shows whose ideas are whose.\n• Messy referencing looks like messy thinking.\n• A source that does not match the list cannot be checked.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your formatting is correct.\n• If a report goes out with errors, you fix the copy on file and your template. You check the list against the text every time.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• APA: American Psychological Association. Its style is a set of rules for referencing.\n• Referencing: showing where your information came from.\n• UCD: University College Dublin.',
    'micro::Say where the evidence is thin':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• For each piece of advice, say how strong the evidence is.\n• Say honestly when evidence is mixed or missing.\n• If there are no trials, give your reasons and a plan to check it for this child.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The PSI Code says do not hide evidence that goes against you.\n• Saying evidence is mixed is better than pretending to be sure.\n• If you overstate it, a school may spend a term on something unlikely to work.\n• They will trust your next report less.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You are honest about the evidence.\n• If you overstated it in a report, you raise it in supervision. You agree a correction.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Evidence base: the research behind an idea.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.',
    'micro::Name the measure':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• For each piece of advice, name how progress will be measured.\n• Pick a test or something you can see and count.\n• Make sure it can change in the time before the review.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• CORU asks you to check plans using known measures and change them if needed.\n• Some measures, like short classroom checks, can change within a term.\n• Yearly tests usually cannot.\n• A measure that cannot change makes the review useless.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You name a specific test or something you can see.\n• If the measure does not work, you keep the data. You agree a better one and record the change.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Measure: a way to check progress.\n• CORU: Ireland's regulator for health and social care professionals.",
    'micro::Name who will measure it':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• For each piece of advice, name who will measure progress.\n• Name a role, like the class teacher or special education teacher.\n• Make sure that person agreed at the meeting.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The school will monitor means no one will.\n• If you name someone who did not agree, the data will not exist at review.\n• A special needs assistant can help only within the limits of their role.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You name a role, not the school.\n• If that person leaves, you contact the school. You agree a new role and record the gap.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Role: a job title, like class teacher.\n• SET: Special Education Teacher.\n• Monitor: keep checking progress.',
    'micro::Name when':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• For each piece of advice, say when progress will be checked.\n• Take a starting measure before help begins.\n• Say how often to check and when to decide if it works.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A review with no date does not happen.\n• Without a starting point, you cannot link any change to the help.\n• Even three checks over a week is enough to start.\n• If the review is after your placement ends, hand it on.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You give a date or a gap between checks.\n• If the date passes with no one there, you set a new one. You do not let it drop.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Baseline: a measure taken before help starts.\n• Review: a meeting to check if help is working.',
    'micro::Say what would count as no progress':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Before help starts, agree what result would mean it is not working.\n• Agree what will happen then.\n• Base this on the starting measure.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Without a line set in advance, any result can be called some progress.\n• Help that is not working can then go on for a year.\n• The point is to agree what failure looks like before you see the data.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You set a level for changing course.\n• If the level turns out wrong, you record that. You reset it with the school using the data you have.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Threshold: the level that tells you to change course.\n• Baseline: a measure taken before help starts.',
    'micro::Follow up whether anyone acted on it':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Go back and check if your advice was acted on.\n• At the review point, contact the named person.\n• Ask if it was done and measured. Compare results with the starting point. Change the plan if needed.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Advice is not the same as it being done.\n• One piece of advice checked and changed is worth more than three dropped.\n• Following up is the only way to learn if your thinking was right.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You asked afterwards.\n• If nothing was done, you do not blame the school. You find out why and bring it to supervision.\n• For reviews after your placement ends, you hand the list on.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Follow up: check back to see what has happened.\n• Recommendation: a piece of advice in a report.',
    'macro::Observation in classroom, playground and clinic':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Watch a child in class, in the playground or in the clinic.\n• Pick your way of watching before you go. There are four main ways.\n• You can write what happens, count behaviour, check at set times, or note what came before and after.\n• Watch the setting and the adults too, not just the child.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• It is the only way to see the child where the problem is really happening.\n• A test in a quiet room does not show this.\n• What a teacher tells you is their view. Watching gives you your own view.\n• Your being there changes how children act. Stay long enough, and say what effect you think you had.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You chose the right way of watching and used it well.\n• You kept what you saw apart from what you think it means.\n• You can say what your method missed. You watched in more than one place.\n• What you saw answers the question you were asked.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Observation: watching carefully and writing down what you see.\n• Referral: a request for a psychologist to help a child.\n• ABC recording: noting what came before, the behaviour, and what came after.\n• WISC-V: a test of thinking skills.',
    'macro::Structured interviewing of parents for developmental history':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Ask a parent about their child's early life in a planned way.\n• Cover pregnancy and birth, movement, speech, social skills, sleep, feeding and self-care.\n• Start by asking what the child is good at.\n• If a parent shares something hard, pause, respond kindly, then gently go back.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• This is the only way to learn about the child's first five years.\n• It turns a snapshot into a full picture.\n• It is also when the parent decides if they can trust you.\n• Rushing it loses both the facts and the trust.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You led the interview yourself.\n• You covered every area, even the uncomfortable ones.\n• When an answer was unclear, like fine, you asked more.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Developmental history: the story of how a child grew and learned from birth.\n• Domain: one area of growth, like speech or movement.\n• Disclosure: when someone tells you something private or serious.",
    'macro::Structured interviewing of teachers and SET':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Ask teachers and special education teachers about a child in a planned way.\n• Find out what has already been tried, and what they noticed.\n• Find out what level of school support the child gets.\n• Turn a vague worry into a clear question you can answer.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Teachers can compare this child with the rest of the class.\n• They know what help has been tried.\n• Without this, your advice may repeat what has already failed.\n• Teachers may expect to be judged. Ask in a way that does not feel like a check.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You made the question clear and knew the support level.\n• You read the school's test results before meeting the child.\n• You came knowing what had been tried. The teacher saw the question as their own.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• SET: Special Education Teacher.\n• Continuum of Support: the three levels of help in Irish schools. These are classroom, school and school support plus.\n• NEPS: National Educational Psychological Service.",
    'macro::WISC-V administration to proficiency standard':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Give the WISC-V test exactly as the manual says.\n• Learn where to start, when to go back, and when to stop.\n• Learn when you may ask a follow-up question, and when you may not.\n• Watch how the child works, not just the scores.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• You will be judged on this test directly. There is an exam on it.\n• Mistakes are easy to see on the record sheet.\n• This test is also often read too much into.\n• Every score has some error. A range is more honest than one number.\n• If parts of a score do not agree, the overall score may not mean much.\n• Learning to give it takes weeks. Learning what it tells you takes years.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You follow the rules exactly.\n• You do not help too much, or carry on past a stop rule to be kind.\n• You report ranges and say what the scores cannot show.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• WISC-V: Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children, fifth edition. A test of thinking skills.\n• CHC: Cattell-Horn-Carroll. The theory of thinking skills the test is built on.\n• Confidence interval: the range where the true score probably lies.',
    'macro::WIAT administration, scoring and interpretation':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Give, score and explain the WIAT test.\n• Pick only the parts that fit the question. Say why you left others out.\n• Score it soon, carefully, and check it.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• This test shows what a child has learned, like reading, spelling and maths.\n• A thinking skills test asks a different question.\n• A gap between the two was once used to diagnose. It is not used that way now.\n• These tests cannot show if help is working week to week. Short, repeated class checks do that better.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You wrote down why you chose the parts you gave.\n• You reported score ranges, not just single numbers.\n• You were careful about gaps and said what they do not prove.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• WIAT: Wechsler Individual Achievement Test. A test of reading, writing and maths.\n• Attainment: what a child has learned so far.\n• Discrepancy: a gap between two scores.\n• Curriculum-based measurement: short, repeated checks of school skills.',
    'macro::Synthesise findings from multiple sources into one profile':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Bring together everything you found into one picture of the child.\n• This includes tests, watching, and what the parent, teacher and child said.\n• Note where sources agree and where they do not.\n• Write it as one joined-up story, not a list of results.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• This is what makes a report more than a list of scores.\n• Most trainee reports lose marks here.\n• When sources agree, that is your strongest evidence.\n• When they do not, you need to explain why.\n• Do not leave out findings that go against your view.\n• Every report should say what it could not cover.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The reader does not have to put it together themselves.\n• You name where sources disagree and explain it.\n• You include a section on limits.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Synthesise: bring many pieces of information together into one picture.\n• Profile: a picture of a child's strengths and needs.\n• Limitations: what an assessment could not show.",
    'macro::Phonological and literacy measures':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Test a child's reading and sound skills.\n• Use a simple model of reading. It has two parts: working out words, and understanding language.\n• Check the child's wider thinking skills before deciding a difficulty is specific.\n• Turn what you find into clear advice the school can use.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Reading worries are the most common referrals in Irish primary schools.\n• Testing only helps if it leads to clear help.\n• You need to know what help schools near you can really give.\n• Phonics will not help a child whose problem is understanding.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You give and score the tests correctly.\n• You read the results alongside the child's thinking skills.\n• Your advice follows clearly from what you found.\n• The teacher could start the help on Monday.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Phonological skills: hearing and working with the sounds in words.\n• Decoding: working out what written words say.\n• Phonics: teaching the links between letters and sounds.\n• SET: Special Education Teacher.",
    'macro::Social communication screening measures':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Use short checklists that look for signs of social communication differences, such as autism.\n• Know that a screen is not a diagnosis.\n• Put the screen together with what you see yourself.\n• Know where a child goes next in your service if the screen is positive.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• In Ireland, diagnosis usually happens through a disability team.\n• Being clear protects the child from a label too early.\n• It also keeps you working within your skills.\n• Parents and your own watching may not agree. That is normal and useful.\n• Girls and some others may hide their difficulties. They are often missed.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You wrote that a screen is not a diagnosis.\n• You thought about hidden difficulties.\n• The family left knowing the next step.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Screening: a quick check to see if more assessment is needed.\n• Diagnosis: a formal decision that a person has a condition.\n• Masking: hiding or covering up difficulties to fit in.\n• CDNT: Children's Disability Network Team.",
    'macro::Behavioural and emotional rating scales':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Use rating forms about a child's behaviour and feelings.\n• Get forms from different people, like a parent and a teacher.\n• Turn scores into plain words.\n• Include the child's own view where you can.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• These forms let two people who see the child in different places rate the same thing.\n• Parents and teachers often disagree. That tells you about the setting, so do not throw it away.\n• A high score means unusual. It does not mean a disorder or a diagnosis.\n• Children have a right to give their own view.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You chose and scored the right form.\n• You explained the scores in plain words.\n• You treated disagreement as useful information.\n• You included the child's own account.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Rating scale: a form where someone rates how often a behaviour happens.\n• Informant: a person who fills in a form about the child.\n• SDQ: Strengths and Difficulties Questionnaire. A short form about behaviour and feelings.",
    'macro::Adapt standardised procedure and record the adaptation':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Change how you give a test when a child needs it.\n• Know which changes keep the score fair and which do not.\n• Write down each change at the moment it happens, and why.\n• Be willing to stop if the test is doing more harm than good.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Scores only compare fairly if the test is given the standard way.\n• But sticking to the rules too tightly can make the test useless for some children.\n• The law says you must make reasonable changes for a person with a disability.\n• In the report, say what you changed and how it affects the score.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your changes fit the need and were noted at the time.\n• You said how they affect the results.\n• You were willing to stop, and said so in writing.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Standardised: done the same way every time so scores can be compared.\n• Adaptation: a change to how a test is given.\n• Valid: results you can trust.\n• Reasonable accommodation: fair changes for a person with a disability.',
    'macro::Learn the four frameworks for practice':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Learn four ways of explaining a child's difficulties.\n• One tests ideas against the evidence. One shows how different levels affect each other.\n• One focuses on change and everyone's point of view. One looks at why a problem started and why it keeps going.\n• Choose the one that fits the question, before you collect data.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A framework is a choice about what counts as an explanation.\n• Without one, you slip into blaming the problem only on the child.\n• Then you cannot explain your thinking if asked.\n• Using the same one every time means you are not really choosing.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can name the four and say what each one leaves out.\n• The question decides which one you use, not your habit.\n• You ask your supervisor which one they use.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Framework: a structure for thinking through a problem.\n• Formulation: an explanation of why a difficulty is happening.\n• IFF: Interactive Factors Framework.\n• COMOIRA: Constructionist Model of Informed and Reasoned Action.",
    'macro::Name and apply one framework to each substantial case':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Use one framework to shape each main report.\n• Let the framework set out the report's sections.\n• Show each step of your thinking, not just the answer.\n• Include the child's own view in your explanation.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Many first-year reports just list findings.\n• A list leaves the reader to work it out.\n• When the framework shapes the report, readers can see how you got your answer.\n• They can disagree at a clear point.\n• Plan the thinking before you start typing.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• A named framework shapes the report and your thinking is clear.\n• The child's view is included.\n• You show where your thinking could be wrong.\n• You say what would change your mind.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Framework: a structure for thinking through a problem.\n• Formulation: an explanation of why a difficulty is happening.\n• CORU: Ireland's regulator for health and social care professionals.",
    'macro::Distinguish primary from secondary needs':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Work out which need came first, and which grew from it.\n• Come up with at least two real explanations. Test each one.\n• Ask when each difficulty started, from more than one person.\n• Check where the problem happens and where it does not.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Schools often refer the need that grew out of another one.\n• For example, bad behaviour may come from a reading difficulty no one spotted.\n• If you only treat what shows on the surface, the help will not work.\n• Sometimes you cannot tell which came first. Then say so honestly.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You test more than one explanation and say which need came first.\n• You write down the explanation you rejected, and why.\n• When it is unclear, you say what would sort it out.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Primary need: the main difficulty that came first.\n• Secondary need: a difficulty that grew out of the main one.\n• Hypothesis: a possible explanation you can test.',
    'macro::Consider the likelihood of co-occurring conditions':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Always check if a child might have more than one condition.\n• Weigh the evidence for each one on its own.\n• Write down what an existing diagnosis does not explain.\n• Describe what you see and refer on. Do not diagnose.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Having more than one condition is common.\n• Once a child has one diagnosis, other needs are often blamed on it. Then they are missed.\n• A very able child with a difficulty can look average. They are often missed too.\n• As a trainee, you do not diagnose conditions like ADHD or autism.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You check for a second condition every time.\n• You say what would be needed to confirm it.\n• You are clear about where describing ends and diagnosing begins.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Co-occurring: happening at the same time.\n• Diagnostic overshadowing: when one diagnosis hides another need.\n• Twice-exceptional: very able and also having a difficulty.\n• ADHD: Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder.',
    'macro::Analyse risk and resilience':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Look at what puts a child at risk, and what protects them.\n• Look at the child, the family and the school.\n• Build at least one piece of advice on a strength.\n• Know the point where a worry becomes a child protection matter.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A report that only lists problems gives nothing to build on.\n• Schools keep up help that uses a real strength.\n• If a child tells you about harm, you stop the assessment. You tell your supervisor that day and follow the DLP route.\n• Telling the DLP is not enough on its own. If you are a mandated person, tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly, with the DLP or yourself.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• One piece of advice is built on a strength you proved.\n• You can say the steps you would follow if a child was at risk.\n• You show how risks and strengths affect each other over time.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Resilience: being able to cope and bounce back.\n• Protective factor: something that helps keep a child safe and well.\n• DLP: Designated Liaison Person. The person who deals with child protection worries.\n• Mandated person: a person the law says must report child protection worries.',
    'macro::Account for the biopsychosocial context':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Look at the child's body and health, their family and culture, and their school.\n• Name the model you are using before you start.\n• Show how these parts affect each other, not just list them.\n• Treat the school as part of the picture, not just the background.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• How you see disability shapes what you advise.\n• If you only look at the child, all your advice will be about the child.\n• Many reports do this by accident, because the tests focus on the child.\n• Things like hearing problems or learning English change how you read the results.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You name your model and show each part playing a role.\n• You show how the parts work together.\n• At least one piece of advice is about changing the setting, not the child.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Biopsychosocial: looking at body, mind and social life together.\n• Medical model: seeing the difficulty as inside the child.\n• Social model: seeing the difficulty as caused by barriers around the person.\n• CORU: Ireland's regulator for health and social care professionals.",
    'macro::Educational interventions in literacy':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Find which part of reading is not working.\n• Parts include sounds, phonics, reading speed, word knowledge and understanding.\n• Pick help that has evidence for that part.\n• Say who gives it, how often, for how long, and how progress is measured.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The school already knows the child cannot read.\n• They need to know what exactly to do about it.\n• Help must follow from your assessment, not just from what the school owns.\n• Take a starting measure, and use a measure that can change within a term.\n• Some things sold to schools, like coloured overlays, lack good evidence.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You name the weak part and matching help, with how much and who.\n• You set a measure and a review point.\n• You can say what you would do if nothing changes after a term.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Intervention: a planned way of helping.\n• Phonics: teaching the links between letters and sounds.\n• Fluency: reading smoothly and at a good speed.\n• SET: Special Education Teacher.',
    'macro::Educational interventions in numeracy':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Find where a child is in learning about number.\n• Number skills build up in steps, from counting to working out sums.\n• Work out why a child struggles with maths. It could be reading, memory, number sense, method or worry.\n• Name the level of school support the child needs.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Maths gets less attention than reading.\n• Knowing the steps lets you say where the child is, not just that they are behind.\n• A teacher can act on that.\n• The evidence for maths help is weaker than for reading. Be honest about that.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You place the child in the steps and find the cause.\n• You name the support level.\n• You give the evidence and its limits.\n• You describe the teaching approach, not just a programme name.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Numeracy: skills with number and maths.\n• Cardinality: knowing the last number you count tells you how many there are.\n• Continuum of Support: the three levels of help in Irish schools.',
    'macro::Wellbeing interventions at class and group level':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Plan and run wellbeing work with a whole class or a small group.\n• Choose whether to work with everyone, a group at risk, or one child.\n• Pick who joins a group with care.\n• Work with school staff from the start, so they can carry it on.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Group work can reach many children for the cost of one assessment.\n• Programmes only work if they are run as planned.\n• A badly planned group can do harm. Grouping children with similar behaviour problems can make it worse.\n• Work that stops when you leave has failed.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You chose the level on purpose and picked group members carefully.\n• You ran it with school staff.\n• You measured before and after, and checked it was run as planned.\n• You handed it over and can say what you would change.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Universal: for everyone in the class or school.\n• Targeted: for a group at risk.\n• Fidelity: running a programme the way it was designed.\n• SEL: Social and Emotional Learning.',
    'macro::Foundations in counselling skills':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Use basic listening skills in meetings with parents, children and teachers.\n• Ask open questions. Reflect back what people say and feel.\n• Offer feelings gently, like that sounds frightening, rather than labelling them.\n• Know clearly where your skills end.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• You are not a therapist in Year 1.\n• But every meeting uses these skills.\n• Without them, you get less information and weaker relationships.\n• You can listen and help someone think. You cannot give therapy or treat mental health problems.\n• If a parent shares their own mental health needs, listen and point them to help.\n• End clearly. Say what happens next and what will be written down.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You reflect feelings gently and can sit with silence.\n• You can say where your skills end.\n• You notice when a talk goes beyond your skills, and refer on cleanly.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Reflection: saying back what someone told you, to show you understood.\n• Signpost: point someone to the right service.\n• Competence: having the skills to do a task safely.',
    'macro::Write recommendations naming the Continuum of Support level':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• In your advice, name the level of school support the child needs.\n• The three levels are Classroom Support, School Support and School Support Plus.\n• Say why it is that level.\n• Give each piece of advice to a named role, with how often and how long.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Irish schools use these levels to share out help.\n• If you do not name a level, the school cannot act on your advice.\n• Fewer, clear pieces of advice are more likely to happen.\n• Each one needs a review date and a way to measure it.\n• Write so parents and teachers can each find their part.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You name and explain the level.\n• Your advice is short, in order, and each part has a review point.\n• A parent and a teacher can each act on it.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Continuum of Support: the three levels of help in Irish schools.\n• SET: Special Education Teacher.\n• SENO: Special Educational Needs Organiser.',
    'macro::Observe and deliver parental feedback sessions':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Watch, then lead, meetings where you share results with parents.\n• Start by asking what they already know and want.\n• Give the main finding early, in plain words.\n• Turn scores into meaning. Do not lead with numbers.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• This is where the assessment becomes real for the family.\n• A confused parent will not follow the advice.\n• A parent who feels judged may pull away from the school.\n• If a parent cries, gets angry or disagrees, pause and listen. You do not need to fix it.\n• Think carefully about who from the school is in the room.\n• With an interpreter, speak to the parent, not the interpreter.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You plan the meeting, explain scores in plain words and check understanding.\n• You stay calm when it gets hard.\n• You work well through an interpreter.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Feedback session: a meeting to share assessment results.\n• Percentile: a way of comparing a child with others the same age.\n• Interpreter: a person who translates spoken language.',
    'macro::Observe and then contribute to teacher consultation':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Watch, then take part in, planned talks with teachers about a child.\n• Learn one way of doing this well, and use it again and again.\n• Spend most of the time making the problem clear.\n• Ask the teacher what they think is going on.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• This is how one psychologist helps more children than they could ever assess.\n• Irish educational psychologists spend most of their time on it.\n• A problem like he is disruptive needs to become something clear and countable.\n• If you just hand over answers, the teacher owns them less and drops them sooner.\n• Write down who does what by when, and set a review date.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You use a named model and take time to define the problem.\n• You record actions and review dates.\n• You stay out of the expert role, and the teacher's thinking shifts.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Consultation: working with a teacher to solve a problem together.\n• BPS: British Psychological Society.",
    'macro::Familiarise with SET provision and allocation':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Learn how special education teaching hours are given to schools.\n• Learn who decides how they are used.\n• Learn what special needs assistants are for.\n• Read the school's own support files and policy.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Hours go to the school as a block, not to each child.\n• A diagnosis does not bring extra hours.\n• Special needs assistants help with care needs, not with learning.\n• If you ask for things the school cannot give, you lose trust.\n• Rules change. Check the current Department circular.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can explain how hours are shared out.\n• You know the roles of the SENCO, the principal and the SENO.\n• Your advice fits the system.\n• You use the gap between what is written and what really happens as a finding.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• SET: Special Education Teacher.\n• SNA: Special Needs Assistant.\n• SENCO: Special Educational Needs Coordinator.\n• SENO: Special Educational Needs Organiser.\n• SEN: Special Educational Needs.",
    "macro::Map the school's organisation of supports":
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Find out how a school organises its extra support.\n• Learn who is on the team, how children are picked, group sizes and how plans are shared.\n• Ask in a curious way, not like an inspection.\n• Share what you find without blame.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Two schools with the same hours can get very different results.\n• The difference is how support is organised.\n• Sometimes the problem is how help is given, not the child.\n• Then help for just one child will not fix it.\n• Offer one change, not five.\n• Log this as systemic work, not as casework.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You gathered it carefully and used it in your thinking.\n• You found something about how help is delivered.\n• You shared it in a way the school could act on.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Systemic work: working to change how a school or service works, not just helping one child.\n• SEN: Special Educational Needs.\n• SET: Special Education Teacher.',
    'macro::Visit a range of special education settings':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Visit a range of special education settings.\n• These include special classes, special schools and early years classes.\n• Look at staffing, what is taught, how people communicate and the sensory setting.\n• Notice your own views about special education.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• You will be asked if a child needs a special class or school.\n• You cannot answer honestly if you have never seen one.\n• The course requires this experience. You do not meet it yet.\n• The question is about fit between the child and the setting, not how severe the need is.\n• Ask in week one and put it in your plan, or it will not happen.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You visited a range and can describe how they differ.\n• You can write advice about fit between need and setting.\n• You can name your own views and how you checked them.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Special class: a small class for children with special needs in an ordinary school.\n• Mainstream: an ordinary school or class.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.',
    'macro::Attend service team meetings':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Go to your service's team meetings and take part.\n• Bring one small thing to offer each time.\n• Learn how the service works, from referral to closing a case.\n• In meetings with other professions, share your view in plain words.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Team meetings show you what the service really is.\n• They are a chance to practise speaking up among colleagues.\n• Knowing waiting times and priorities makes your advice realistic.\n• As a trainee, ask questions or offer help. Do not judge how the service is run.\n• Different professions use the same words for different things.\n• Log what you gave, not just that you went.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You prepare something to share.\n• You can describe how referrals move through the service.\n• You explain your thinking in words other professions can use.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Multi-disciplinary: with people from different professions.\n• Referral pathway: the route a request for help takes through a service.\n• CORU: Ireland's regulator for health and social care professionals.",
    'macro::Learn consultation models':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Learn the main ways of working with teachers to solve problems.\n• One follows steps from defining the problem to checking results.\n• One looks at what already works and what people want.\n• Others treat you and the teacher, or parent and teacher, as equal partners.\n• Learn one well this year and use it again and again.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• This is the main way Irish educational psychologists work.\n• Without a model, you tend to just give advice. That is often ignored.\n• Most failures come from agreeing on the problem too fast.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You use one model on purpose and name it in your records.\n• You can say what your model is poor at.\n• You change approach when the situation needs it.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Consultation: working with a teacher to solve a problem together.\n• Model: a set way of doing something.\n• Solution-focused: looking at what already works and building on it.\n• BPS: British Psychological Society.',
    'macro::Deliver the good practice presentation':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Plan and give a training talk to school staff.\n• Pick a topic that comes from a pattern you saw in your cases.\n• End each part with what to do, not just what to know.\n• Leave a one-page summary behind.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Training reaches more people than working with one child.\n• Your supervisor said this is one of your strengths.\n• Teachers want something they can use on Monday.\n• If you say something works, give a source. Do not overclaim.\n• A short form at the end shows what people will do differently. It gives you evidence.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your topic came from practice and suited the audience.\n• You left a resource behind.\n• You checked how it went and can say what you would change.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Evaluate: check how well something worked.\n• Practice-based research: learning from checking your own work.\n• CORU: Ireland's regulator for health and social care professionals.",
    'macro::Build rapport with a child during assessment':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Help a child feel at ease during an assessment.\n• In the first few minutes, say who you are, why you are there and what will happen.\n• Tell them some tasks get hard for everyone.\n• Say what happens to the information.\n• Watch for tiredness or worry, and use the breaks the manual allows.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Rapport is not just being polite.\n• A worried or bored child will score lower than they really are.\n• Then your whole report rests on a wrong number.\n• If a child refuses, do not push. Stopping and coming back is better.\n• At the end, thank them and say what happens next.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You explain things in words the child understands.\n• You notice how they are and adjust within the rules.\n• You record any change and what it means.\n• You can explain why you stopped, if you did.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Rapport: a good, trusting connection with someone.\n• Validity: whether results can be trusted.\n• Confidentiality: keeping what someone tells you private.',
    'macro::Communicate with school staff, SENCO and principal':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Work well with teachers, the SENCO and the principal.\n• Know who does what in the school.\n• Confirm visits, arrive on time and keep to the time you asked for.\n• After a meeting, send a short written summary within a day.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The school decides if your advice happens.\n• If you are clear and easy to work with, advice gets used.\n• If you are late or vague, it gets filed away.\n• Sometimes the problem is how the school gives support. Share this kindly and offer one change.\n• If a teacher disagrees, ask what they see. They may be right.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You go to the right person for each thing.\n• You follow up in writing.\n• You can share a hard message and keep the relationship. The school acts on it.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• SENCO: Special Educational Needs Coordinator.\n• SET: Special Education Teacher.\n• SNA: Special Needs Assistant.',
    'macro::Give feedback to parents on assessment findings':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Explain assessment results to parents.\n• Give the main finding plainly in the first couple of minutes.\n• Use everyday words instead of test words.\n• Start with a real strength you found.\n• Check what they understood by asking them to explain it back.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A parent who understands can speak up for their child.\n• That can matter more over the years than any one piece of advice.\n• Made-up strengths sound like comfort and weaken your message.\n• If a parent disagrees, ask what they see. They know their child far better.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You lead with the main finding and use plain words.\n• You check understanding.\n• You treat disagreement as useful.\n• The parent can explain the findings to someone else.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Feedback: sharing results with someone.\n• Percentile: a way of comparing a child with others the same age.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.',
    'macro::Keep case notes to service standard':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Write case notes the same day, to your service's standard.\n• Include who, what, where, when, what was decided and what happens next.\n• Keep what you saw apart from what you think it means.\n• Store notes safely. Never keep them on a personal device.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Case notes are a legal record.\n• Families can ask to see them. A court may too.\n• Write as if the family will read them.\n• Notes written on the day take minutes.\n• Notes written later take hours and it shows.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You write notes the same day.\n• You separate facts from opinions.\n• Your notes would stand up if a family asked to see them.\n• You can say the storage rules you follow.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Case notes: written records of your work with a child.\n• GDPR: General Data Protection Regulation. The law on personal information.\n• Subject access request: when a person asks to see the records held about them.\n• NEPS: National Educational Psychological Service.",
    'macro::Produce the psychological report to countersigning standard':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Write a psychology report your supervisor is willing to sign.\n• Use a clear structure: the question, what you did, what you found, your explanation, advice, limits and a review point.\n• Answer the question you were asked.\n• Show where your sources agree and where they do not.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The report lasts long after you leave.\n• Parents, teachers and others read it, sometimes years later.\n• Your supervisor puts their name to your thinking.\n• Limits should be specific to this child, not general.\n• Treat your supervisor's changes as teaching. Ask which were about your thinking.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• It answers the question and uses a named framework.\n• It has specific limits and a review point.\n• It joins up the sources, even where they conflict.\n• It only needs small style changes.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Countersign: when a senior person signs to approve your work.\n• Formulation: an explanation of why a difficulty is happening.\n• Limitations: what a report cannot show.",
    'macro::Adapt written communication to the audience':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Write so each reader can use your report.\n• Readers include parents, teachers, the SENCO and the SENO.\n• Put a plain summary at the top, detail in the middle, and clear advice at the end.\n• Write the summary last, and remove any word you have not explained.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• One style of writing cannot suit every reader.\n• Each reader has their own question.\n• Most people read only the first page and the advice.\n• If the report goes through an interpreter, use short sentences and no sayings.\n• Test the advice on someone outside psychology. If they cannot say what to do first, rewrite it.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your report is layered, with plain advice for each role.\n• You adapt for reading level and language.\n• You note in the report that you did this.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• SENCO: Special Educational Needs Coordinator.\n• SENO: Special Educational Needs Organiser.\n• CORU: Ireland's regulator for health and social care professionals.",
    'macro::Garda vetting and Children First certification':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Get Garda vetting before placement.\n• Do the Children First online training and keep the certificate.\n• Learn what it means to be a mandated person.\n• Get the DLP's name for every school you work in.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Without vetting, you cannot be alone with a child.\n• Vetting may not carry over to a new service. Check.\n• Without training, you do not know your legal duties.\n• As a mandated person, the law says you must report harm above a set level. You tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly.\n• Telling the school's DLP is not enough on its own.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Vetting and training are done and kept on file.\n• You can say what a mandated person must do, and when to report.\n• You know the DLP for every school on your list.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Garda vetting: a police check before working with children.\n• Mandated person: a person the law says must report child protection worries.\n• DLP: Designated Liaison Person. The person in a school who deals with child protection worries.",
    'macro::Complete service induction':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Complete a full induction in each new service.\n• Learn the computer systems, where files are kept, and how referrals come in.\n• Learn the safety rules, including working alone and travel.\n• Read the actual policies. Do not just be told about them.\n• Write a one-page note of what you learned.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Induction teaches the things everyone assumes you know.\n• Skipping it just moves the cost to the day something goes wrong.\n• You will drive alone and sometimes visit homes. Know who knows where you are.\n• If something happens, you are held to the written policy.\n• A new service means a new induction.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You have the policies and know the referral route.\n• You recorded your induction.\n• You can say the lone-working and risk rules without looking.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Induction: the start-up training in a new workplace.\n• Lone working: working on your own, away from colleagues.\n• CORU: Ireland's regulator for health and social care professionals.",
    'macro::Obtain informed consent and assent':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Get informed consent from parents, and assent from the child.\n• Explain the purpose, what will happen, risks, benefits and other options.\n• Say they can say no or stop at any time without losing help.\n• Say who asked for the work, who will see the report, and what you cannot keep private.\n• Ask again if the work changes.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Consent is a process, not a signature. The form only records it.\n• It goes on the whole time.\n• An assessment without real consent should not have happened.\n• Watch for a child showing they want to stop without words.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You go through what must be understood.\n• You get the child's assent and explain the limits of privacy.\n• You ask again when the purpose changes.\n• You can name the Code rules behind each step.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Informed consent: saying yes after being told all the important facts.\n• Assent: a child's own agreement to take part.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.",
    'macro::Recognise and act on child protection concerns':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Notice signs that a child may be harmed.\n• There are four types: neglect, emotional, physical and sexual abuse.\n• If a child tells you, listen. Do not promise secrecy. Do not ask leading questions.\n• Stop, write down their words and the time, and tell your supervisor that day.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Getting this wrong cannot be undone.\n• You do not need to be sure. You need reasonable grounds for concern.\n• Follow the school's DLP route. As a mandated person, you must also tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly, if the level is met.\n• Telling the DLP is not enough on its own. This duty is yours.\n• Do not wait for supervision. Afterwards, bring it to supervision anyway.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can name the types, the level for reporting and every step.\n• You have followed it in practice and recorded it.\n• You used supervision afterwards.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Disclosure: when a child tells you about harm.\n• DLP: Designated Liaison Person. The person who deals with child protection worries.\n• Mandated person: a person the law says must report.",
    'macro::Work an ethical dilemma through a decision procedure':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Work through a hard ethical choice step by step.\n• Use the seven steps in the PSI Code.\n• Name everyone affected, find the rules that clash, and list many options.\n• Choose, act, and take responsibility for what happens.\n• Keep notes at each step.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Being ethical is not just good instinct.\n• It means showing you followed a clear process when the answer was not obvious.\n• A real dilemma is when two duties clash, like privacy and safety.\n• The best option is often not one of the first two.\n• A written dilemma is strong evidence for your portfolio.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You follow the steps and name the rules in tension.\n• You name everyone affected and find real options.\n• You write it down and can explain what happened after.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Ethical dilemma: when two duties pull in different directions.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.\n• Appendix 8: the course form where you write up dilemmas.',
    'macro::State and hold the limits of your competence':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Know what you are not able to do yet, and say so.\n• As a trainee, you do not diagnose, give therapy, work with trauma or advise on medicine.\n• You do not use tests you have not been trained on, or give scores on the day.\n• Say the limit, offer what you can do, and name who can help.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• In Year 1, the pressure pushes you to do more than you should.\n• Saying no can feel like failure. It is not. It is required.\n• The pull is strongest when someone is upset and you are the only professional there.\n• Trying new things under supervision is how you grow. That is fine.\n• If your judgement is badly affected, you must step back and get advice.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can state your limits and say no with another option.\n• You notice the pull in the moment.\n• You learn new skills under supervision.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Competence: having the skills to do a task safely.\n• Impaired: not able to think or work safely right now.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.',
    'macro::Manage workload and your own wellbeing':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Plan work you can keep up across a whole term.\n• Count travel, the visit, the write-up and the follow-up.\n• Block time for writing, like it was a visit.\n• When saying no, give a reason and another option.\n• Use the supports agreed for you. Agree them in writing early.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• CORU and the PSI Code say workload and your health are part of your job.\n• Your review named workload and self-care as an area to work on.\n• Visits back to back with no writing time lead to a backlog.\n• Your supervisor often does not know how full you are.\n• If a problem is built into your timetable, raise it in writing. Do not just put up with it.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You plan realistically and talk about workload with clear facts.\n• Your supports are agreed early.\n• You watch how you are doing and raise bigger problems the right way.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Workload: all the work you have to do.\n• Self-care: things you do to look after your wellbeing.\n• CORU: Ireland's regulator for health and social care professionals.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.",
    'macro::Agree and sign the supervision contract':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Agree a written supervision contract with your supervisor in week one.\n• Write in the exact day and time of the weekly hour.\n• Say what happens if it is missed, who covers when your supervisor is away, and the dates you will be watched.\n• Ask for what helps you work well, in a practical way.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The contract makes everything else something you can ask for.\n• Without it, the weekly hour is a favour that gets cancelled when work is busy.\n• The course sets out what you are due. This includes weekly supervision, being watched both ways and review meetings.\n• A new placement means a new contract. The old one does not carry over.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You shaped the contract, not just signed a template.\n• It names times, cover and dates.\n• You can name the rule behind each thing you are due.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Supervision contract: a written agreement about how supervision will work.\n• Supervisor: the qualified psychologist who guides and checks your work.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.',
    'macro::Take the weekly supervision hour':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Keep your weekly supervision hour happening.\n• Put it in the calendar as a repeat meeting. Avoid the last hour of the day.\n• If it moves, rebook it in the same week before you leave the talk.\n• Bring about three real items. Log informal contact too.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The weekly hour protects you more than anything else on placement.\n• It is also the first thing to disappear, bit by bit.\n• Supervision asks what you were thinking and what you might be missing.\n• That is different from just updating on cases.\n• Supervision can fall short without anyone being to blame. Raise it early.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You protect the hour, come prepared and log all contact.\n• You can tell supervision from case updates, and steer back when it drifts.\n• You raise a shortfall early, the right way.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Supervision: regular meetings with a senior psychologist to guide your work.\n• Case management: sorting out what happens next on cases.\n• Informal contact: short, unplanned talks with your supervisor.',
    'macro::Prepare and send an agenda in advance':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Send your supervisor an agenda by email the day before.\n• List a few items, in order of importance.\n• Show your own thinking on each one, and where you are stuck.\n• Mark what you want from each item: a decision, a view or just to share.\n• Include the hard item. End with last week's actions.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Your supervisor comes ready, so the hour starts with what matters.\n• The email is a dated record of what you raised.\n• The thing you want to avoid is usually the most useful item.\n• Once it is written down, you will raise it.\n• Carrying actions forward makes the sessions build on each other.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• An agenda goes every week, in advance.\n• Items show your thinking and say what you want.\n• The hard item is on there.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Agenda: a list of things to talk about at a meeting.\n• Action: a task agreed at a meeting.\n• CORU: Ireland's regulator for health and social care professionals.",
    'macro::Keep supervision session records':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Write a record of each supervision session the same day.\n• Note the date, who was there, what you talked about, what was decided and what you will do next.\n• Tag each record with the skill areas it covered.\n• Use case numbers, never children's names.\n• Write a longer record for an ethical dilemma. Get records signed as you go.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• These records are marked by the course.\n• They are the only proof you got the supervision you were due.\n• Written on the day, they take about ten minutes.\n• Written months later, it shows.\n• Over time, they show how much you have grown.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Records are written the same day and tagged.\n• You have at least one full dilemma record.\n• Records are signed as you go and show growth over time.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Appendix 8: the course form for supervision records.\n• Countersign: when your supervisor signs your record.\n• Ethical dilemma: a hard choice about the right thing to do.",
    'macro::Observe your supervisor with a client':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Watch your supervisor work with a family or child.\n• Ask in week one for something specific, like a feedback meeting.\n• Watch how they handle the room and the hard moments.\n• Write down their exact words.\n• Ask them afterwards why they made certain choices.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• You learn more from watching an expert in a hard moment than from reading.\n• You learn things nobody writes down.\n• The course says watching must go both ways.\n• Trainees often skip watching their supervisor.\n• Fill in the form on the day, not months later.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You watched with a focus and talked it through after.\n• You noted how they worked, not just what they did.\n• You can name skills you picked up and where you have used them since.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Observe: watch someone work so you can learn.\n• Debrief: a talk after an event about what happened.\n• Appendix 7: the course form for observations.',
    'macro::Be observed with a client':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Have your supervisor watch you work with a family or child.\n• Book the first time in your first month.\n• Tell them what to watch for. Choose something you find hard.\n• Listen to feedback without arguing. Then act on it.\n• Fill in your part of the form on the same day.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Being watched is the only way anyone sees what you really do.\n• Reports and supervision only give your own account.\n• Early feedback leaves time to change.\n• Asking for any feedback often gets only praise.\n• Coming back the next week with what you tried is what matters.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You named a focus and filled in your section on the day.\n• You acted on the feedback.\n• You chose something hard and can show a change after.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Observation: when someone watches you work.\n• Feedback: comments on how you did.\n• Appendix 7: the course form for observations.',
    'macro::Raise immediate concerns out of session':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Raise serious worries with your supervisor the same day. Do not wait for your next session.\n• Serious worries include a child at risk, a child telling you about harm, an injury, or a mistake you may have made.\n• Stop what you are doing. Do not question the child.\n• Write down what was said, with the time. Follow the school's DLP route.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Trainees often do not want to interrupt.\n• If you wonder whether it is serious enough, it is.\n• Following the DLP route is not enough on its own. If you are a mandated person, tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly, with the DLP or yourself.\n• If you cannot reach your supervisor, you still act. Write down every call you tried.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can say what counts and have the DLP route ready.\n• You have followed the steps and recorded them.\n• You wrote it up as a dilemma entry afterwards.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• DLP: Designated Liaison Person. The person who deals with child protection worries.\n• Mandated person: a person the law says must report.",
    'macro::Contribute to peer and group supervision':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Go to peer supervision with other trainees every time.\n• Present one of your own cases, with the part where you are stuck.\n• Help others by asking questions, not giving quick answers.\n• Keep all cases private. Take out names and schools.\n• Log each session.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• You see what other trainees face.\n• You learn your difficulties are normal.\n• You practise presenting a case to people who may challenge you.\n• Peer groups often stop when people get busy. Turning up is most of the skill.\n• If you do not log sessions, you cannot show they happened.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You present a case with a clear question and help others.\n• Your questions open cases up.\n• You notice how the group works, and your sessions are logged.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Peer supervision: meeting with other trainees to talk through your work.\n• Anonymised: with names and details taken out.\n• CORU: Ireland's regulator for health and social care professionals.",
    'macro::Complete Appendix 6 before and after':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Rate your own skills before the placement starts.\n• Rate yourself honestly, even if the rating is low.\n• Set a few clear goals you can prove you met.\n• At the end, rate again. Explain each change, and each rating that did not change.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Comparing before and after is the proof of your growth.\n• It only works if the first rating was honest and came first.\n• If you rate high at the start, you have nothing to show at the end.\n• Check the Handbook for the rating scale. Do not assume one.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your first rating is honest.\n• Your goals are clear and can be proved.\n• Ratings that did not change are explained, with a plan.\n• It reads as a real account, not just a form.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Appendix 6: the course form for rating yourself and your reflective essay.\n• Self-evaluation: rating your own skills.\n• Target: a goal you want to reach.',
    'macro::Write the reflective learning essay':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Write an essay about how you changed over the year.\n• Read what the Handbook asks for before you start.\n• Write about real difficulties, but keep the focus on your work.\n• Show change with dated examples.\n• Use your supervision records.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The essay turns your forms into one clear story of the year.\n• Trainees often write what happened, not what changed in them.\n• CORU asks you to think about your own values, feelings and self-care.\n• A short weekly note makes the essay easier to write.\n• You do not need to share private matters.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You show change with dated, clear examples.\n• You are honest about what was hard.\n• You name how your own values shaped your work.\n• You say what you would do differently next time.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Reflective essay: writing about what you learned and how you changed.\n• Bias: a view you hold that can affect your judgement.\n• CORU: Ireland's regulator for health and social care professionals.",
    'macro::Search and critically appraise evidence':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Find research that helps answer a question about a child.\n• Look first for reviews that sum up many studies.\n• Then search a research database with a clear question.\n• Judge how good each study is: how it was done, who took part, and how big the effect was.\n• Keep a note of what you searched and found.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Every piece of advice is a claim about what will help.\n• If you cannot find and judge the evidence, you are advising from habit.\n• A result can be statistically real but too small to be worth a school's time.\n• Some weak evidence looks strong, like studies run by the people who sell the programme.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You search reviews first and judge studies properly.\n• You reject weak evidence.\n• You can say how sure the evidence lets you be.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Appraise: judge how good something is.\n• Effect size: how big a change a programme made.\n• EEF: Education Endowment Foundation.\n• CORU: Ireland's regulator for health and social care professionals.",
    'macro::Cite evidence for recommendations':
        'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Give sources for your main advice in reports.\n• Cite reviews of many studies rather than one study.\n• Say how strong the evidence is, including when it is mixed.\n• If there is no research for something, say so. Give your reasons and a plan to check it.\n• Use the APA 7th style correctly.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A source shows the advice is not just your opinion.\n• It lets readers check it.\n• The PSI Code says do not hide evidence that goes against you, or overstate what works.\n• Two or three good sources is right for a school report.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Key advice has sources, set out correctly.\n• You state how strong the evidence is.\n• Advice with no research behind it has reasons and a plan to check it.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Cite: name the source of an idea.\n• APA: American Psychological Association. Its style is a set of rules for referencing.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.',
    'macro::State how a recommendation would be evaluated':
        "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• For each piece of advice, say how you will know if it worked.\n• Say what will be measured, when, how and by whom.\n• Pick a measure that can change in the time you have.\n• Take a starting measure before help begins.\n• Go back and check, then change the plan if needed.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Advice that is never checked is just a guess.\n• CORU and the PSI Code say you must check the effect of your work.\n• Without a starting measure, you cannot link any change to the help.\n• Simple before and after checks on one child are a fair way to do small research on placement.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Each piece of advice has a measure, a review date and a named person.\n• You take starting measures.\n• You go back to review and change the advice based on the evidence.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Baseline: a measure taken before help starts.\n• Evaluate: check how well something worked.\n• CORU: Ireland's regulator for health and social care professionals.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.",
    'competency::1. Assessment':
        "WHAT IT MEANS\n• Assessment means finding out about a child's strengths and needs.\n• You use tests, watching, and talking with the child, parents and teachers.\n• It has ten main skills, from watching in class to changing a test fairly when needed.\nWHAT IT LOOKS LIKE\n• Year 1: you learn the tests and give them correctly. Another psychologist could check your scoring and get the same numbers.\n• Year 2: you choose which tests to use. You start assessing in health settings.\n• Year 3: you assess in complex and high-risk cases. You can explain when not to assess at all.\nWHAT YOU DO\n• Give tests exactly as the manual says.\n• Bring all your findings together into one picture.\n• Record any change you make to a test, and why.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Assessment: finding out about a person's strengths and needs.\n• Standardised test: a test given the same way every time so scores can be compared.",
    'competency::2. Formulation':
        "WHAT IT MEANS\n• Formulation means explaining why a child's difficulty is happening.\n• It pulls together everything you found.\n• It has six main skills. These include using a framework, finding the main need, and looking at risks and strengths.\nWHAT IT LOOKS LIKE\n• Year 1: you learn four frameworks. You use one clearly in each main case and name it in the report.\n• Year 2: you explain cases where a child has more than one need. You use information from other professions. The explanation becomes the main argument of your report.\n• Year 3: you explain mental health difficulties and high-risk cases. You hold several possible explanations at once.\nWHAT YOU DO\n• Choose a framework that fits the question.\n• Test more than one explanation.\n• Show your thinking so others can follow it.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Formulation: an explanation of why a difficulty is happening.\n• Framework: a structure for thinking through a problem.",
    'competency::3. Intervention':
        'WHAT IT MEANS\n• Intervention means planning and giving help that makes a difference.\n• It covers reading, maths, wellbeing groups, listening skills, advice, and meetings with parents and teachers.\n• It has seven main skills.\nWHAT IT LOOKS LIKE\n• Year 1: you watch others give help, then give simple help yourself. Your advice names the right level of school support and gives evidence.\n• Year 2: you plan, give and check help that is based on evidence. This includes positive behaviour support and talking therapy with young people.\n• Year 3: you work in a therapeutic way in complex settings. You use several therapy approaches. You check whole programmes.\nWHAT YOU DO\n• Match help to what you found.\n• Say who gives it, how often and how progress is measured.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Intervention: a planned way of helping.\n• Continuum of Support: the three levels of help in Irish schools.\n• CBT: Cognitive Behavioural Therapy. A talking therapy about thoughts, feelings and actions.',
    'competency::4. Systemic Change':
        'WHAT IT MEANS\n• Systemic change means helping whole schools and services, not just one child.\n• It has six main skills. These include learning how support is organised, visiting special settings, team meetings, working with teachers and giving training.\nWHAT IT LOOKS LIKE\n• Year 1: you learn how a school organises support and how a service is set up. This is mostly watching and mapping.\n• Year 2: you work with classes, schools, teams and families. You run groups and training.\n• Year 3: you help bring about change. You build working links across organisations. You work at a whole-system level on mental health needs.\nWHAT YOU DO\n• Learn how the school and service really work.\n• Look for changes that help many children.\n• Log this work as systemic, not as casework.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Systemic: about the whole system, like a school or service.\n• Organisation: a group such as a school or service.',
    'competency::5. Working with Others':
        "WHAT IT MEANS\n• Working with others means building good links with children, families, schools and other professionals.\n• It has six main skills. These include rapport with children, talking with school staff, feedback to parents, case notes, reports and writing for each reader.\nWHAT IT LOOKS LIKE\n• Year 1: you build rapport and keep case notes to your service's standard. You write a report your supervisor will sign.\n• Year 2: you change how you communicate for health and disability settings. You take part in teams with other professions.\n• Year 3: you lead communication in complex cases. This includes when professionals and families disagree.\nWHAT YOU DO\n• Use plain words.\n• Listen, especially when people disagree.\n• Keep clear, timely records.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Rapport: a good, trusting connection with someone.\n• Countersign: when a senior person signs to approve your work.\n• Multi-disciplinary: with people from different professions.",
    'competency::6. Ethical Practice':
        "WHAT IT MEANS\n• Ethical practice means working safely, fairly and within the rules of your profession.\n• It has seven main skills. These include vetting, induction, consent, child protection, working through dilemmas, knowing your limits and managing workload.\nWHAT IT LOOKS LIKE\n• Year 1: you get vetted and inducted. You take consent properly and know the child protection route. Following the rules comes first.\n• Year 2: you work through hard choices using the steps in the PSI Code. You keep to your limits under pressure.\n• Year 3: you manage risk in high-risk settings. You speak up when an organisation's way of working clashes with the Code.\nWHAT YOU DO\n• Follow the Code and the law.\n• Say what you cannot do yet.\n• Act quickly on child protection worries.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Ethics: rules and ideas about the right way to act.\n• Vetting: a police check before working with children.\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.",
    'competency::7. Supervision':
        'WHAT IT MEANS\n• Supervision means regular meetings with a senior psychologist who guides and checks your work.\n• It has ten main skills. These include the contract, the weekly hour, agendas, records, watching and being watched, peer groups, rating yourself and your reflective essay.\nWHAT IT LOOKS LIKE\n• Year 1: you build the habits. These are the contract, the weekly hour, the agenda, records, being watched, your self-rating form and the essay.\n• Year 2: you use supervision to look at your own reactions, not just your cases. Peer supervision becomes more real.\n• Year 3: you think about supervision as a future supervisor would. You can explain the supervision framework you work within.\nWHAT YOU DO\n• Protect the weekly hour.\n• Come prepared, and bring what went badly.\n• Keep records and get them signed.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Supervision: regular meetings with a senior psychologist to guide your work.\n• Peer supervision: meeting with other trainees to talk through your work.\n• Appendix 6: the course form for rating yourself.',
    'competency::8. Research':
        'WHAT IT MEANS\n• Research means using evidence to guide your work, and adding to it.\n• It has three main skills. These are finding and judging evidence, citing it, and saying how advice will be checked.\nWHAT IT LOOKS LIKE\n• Year 1: you search for, judge and cite evidence for the advice you write.\n• Year 2: you plan and run checks on your own work. You carry your thesis through data collection.\n• Year 3: you finish your long thesis and defend it at a viva. You find ways to share what you found.\nWHAT YOU DO\n• Look for good evidence before you advise.\n• Be honest about how strong it is.\n• Check if your advice worked.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Evidence: research or data that shows what works.\n• Thesis: a long piece of research written for your degree.\n• Viva: a spoken exam about your thesis.',
}
